meta {
  name: Commit Campaign (Wrong Status, 409)
  type: http
  seq: 14
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/platform/mailer-campaigns/{{campaignId}}/commit
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{platformAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "mode": "append"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 409 Conflict", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(409);
  });

  test("names the status it refused on", function () {
    // Not a generic "cannot commit": the operator needs to know whether to
    // wait, re-preview, or look at a run that already finished.
    expect(res.getBody().message).to.contain("imported");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /platform/mailer-campaigns/:campaignId/commit — the wrong-status refusal

  The same endpoint as `Commit Campaign (Append)`, sent against the campaign
  that request already imported. See there for the full contract.

  This is the first of the commit gate's checks and the one a client is most
  likely to hit by accident — a stale page, a double-submitted button, a browser
  back. **Only a `previewed` campaign may commit**: committing something still
  parsing would write from a file nobody has seen the shape of, and committing
  something already imported would be a silent double-import (harmless, since
  every write is an upsert, but it would report nonsense).

  The same `409` also covers losing the compare-and-set — two operators looking
  at the same previewed page cannot both start the run — so the message names
  the status rather than describing a single cause.

  ## Response `409`
  ```json
  { "statusCode": 409, "message": "This campaign cannot be committed while it is \"imported\"." }
  ```
}
